WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
How can actin move backward while a crawling cell's edge moves forward?

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.760
How can microtubules slide lengthwise while a cilium bends?

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.620
Both puzzles start with internal motion whose visible output points elsewhere.

4
00:00:15.783 --> 00:00:17.823
Keep both machines on one bench.

5
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:22.143
In each, polymers and motors create internal motion.

6
00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:28.203
Our first picture holds those motion generators steady and changes one constraint.

7
00:00:29.523 --> 00:00:31.203
Then we will ask what it leaves out.

8
00:00:32.183 --> 00:00:36.483
Start at a lamellipodium, the thin actin-rich front of a crawling cell.

9
00:00:38.003 --> 00:00:41.643
Actin subunits extend filaments relative to the network behind.

10
00:00:42.923 --> 00:00:47.683
The substrate is the surface beneath the cell; the edge moves forward relative to

11
00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:48.663
it.

12
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:54.293
Small fluorescent markers called speckles reveal the actin network moving backward

13
00:00:54.293 --> 00:00:55.293
over the substrate.

14
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:58.493
That motion is retrograde flow.

15
00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:05.273
At a lamellipodial edge, polymerization adds forward speed, while retrograde flow

16
00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:11.073
subtracts it. Growth is measured against the network; edge and flow against the

17
00:01:11.073 --> 00:01:12.073
substrate.

18
00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:18.597
An integrin is a membrane receptor that grips extracellular matrix and connects

19
00:01:18.597 --> 00:01:20.577
through linker proteins to actin.

20
00:01:21.797 --> 00:01:26.557
Together they act like a clutch: polymerization is the engine, and adhesion

21
00:01:26.557 --> 00:01:27.817
transmits its motion.

22
00:01:29.177 --> 00:01:33.897
Integrin coupling can reduce backward slip in this simple crawling model, so more

23
00:01:33.897 --> 00:01:35.617
growth appears as edge advance.

24
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.820
Now move to a motile cilium, a hairlike projection that beats.

25
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.260
Its axoneme is the internal microtubule core.

26
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:51.580
In the classic 9+2 layout, nine doublets surround two central microtubules.

27
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.880
Each doublet contains a complete A-tubule fused to an incomplete B-tubule.

28
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.060
In motile axonemes, dynein motors slide neighbouring doublets.

29
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.120
Their arms anchor on one doublet's A-tubule and grip the neighbour's B-tubule.

30
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.160
A head steps toward that neighbour's base.

31
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.480
Seen from the other side, the same action pushes the neighbour toward its tip.

32
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:22.253
In the simplest picture, focus on two neighbouring doublets.

33
00:02:23.413 --> 00:02:25.253
Free doublets would pass one another.

34
00:02:26.893 --> 00:02:29.493
Relative sliding creates shear, a lengthwise offset.

35
00:02:30.873 --> 00:02:36.153
In motile axonemes, links that resist sliding, with base anchoring, turn sliding

36
00:02:36.153 --> 00:02:39.653
into bending. Here the motors stay unchanged.

37
00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:46.067
A real bend needs more: dyneins on opposite sides must not be equally active.

38
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.907
Motor control selects which side is more active.

39
00:02:51.227 --> 00:02:55.147
The constraint shapes the output, while regulation helps choose where the bend

40
00:02:55.147 --> 00:02:56.147
forms.

41
00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.840
Predict both outcomes while the motion generators stay unchanged.

42
00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.940
Release the crawling clutch, and cut the axonemal links.

43
00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.620
What happens to edge advance and to the doublets?

44
00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:19.563
With the clutch released, more actin motion becomes retrograde flow, so the edge

45
00:03:19.563 --> 00:03:21.523
advances less in this simple case.

46
00:03:22.803 --> 00:03:26.323
With links cut, dynein makes doublets slide apart instead of bending.

47
00:03:27.643 --> 00:03:31.783
The motors still work; the changed constraint reveals a different output.

48
00:03:32.867 --> 00:03:36.127
The two machines share a logic, not every detail.

49
00:03:37.527 --> 00:03:40.467
Polymers and motors create internal relative motion.

50
00:03:41.747 --> 00:03:46.487
With motors unchanged, a mechanical constraint helps decide whether motion slips,

51
00:03:46.947 --> 00:03:49.327
advances, slides apart, or bends.

52
00:03:50.787 --> 00:03:55.907
In these two systems, constraints and motor control shape the visible output of

53
00:03:55.907 --> 00:03:56.787
internal motion.
